Subtask 3.4: Test Service Mesh Functionality
Create a test namespace to verify sidecar injection:
oc new-project bookinfo
Add the namespace to the service mesh:
oc patch smmr default -n istio-system --type='json' -p='[{"op": "add", "path": "/spec/members/-", "value": "bookinfo"}]'
Deploy a simple test application:
cat > test-app.yaml << 'EOF'
apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  name: httpbin
  namespace: bookinfo
spec:
  replicas: 1
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: httpbin
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: httpbin
      annotations:
        sidecar.istio.io/inject: "true"
    spec:
      containers:
      - name: httpbin
        image: kennethreitz/httpbin
        ports:
        - containerPort: 80
---
apiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata:
  name: httpbin
  namespace: bookinfo
spec:
  selector:
    app: httpbin
  ports:
  - port: 8000
    targetPort: 80
EOF
Apply the test application:
oc apply -f test-app.yaml
Verify sidecar injection occurred:
oc get pods -n bookinfo
Check that the pod has 2 containers (application + istio-proxy):
oc describe pod -l app=httpbin -n bookinfo
Troubleshooting Tips
Common Installation Issues
Issue: Operators fail to install

Solution: Verify cluster has sufficient resources and proper RBAC permissions
Command: oc describe csv <operator-name> -n <namespace>
Issue: SMCP installation hangs or fails

Solution: Check operator logs and ensure all prerequisite operators are installed
Command: oc logs -f deployment/istio-operator -n openshift-operators
Issue: Pods stuck in pending state

Solution: Check node resources and pod resource requests
Command: oc describe pod <pod-name> -n istio-system
Verification Commands
Check operator health:

oc get csv -A | grep -E "(servicemesh|kiali|jaeger|elasticsearch)"
Monitor SMCP installation progress:

watch oc get smcp basic -n istio-system
View detailed SMCP status:

oc describe smcp basic -n istio-system
Conclusion
In this lab, you have successfully:

• Installed all required Service Mesh operators using the Operator Lifecycle Manager, including Elasticsearch, Jaeger, Kiali, and the main Service Mesh operator • Created and configured a Service Mesh Control Plane (SMCP) with essential components like Istio, Jaeger for tracing, and Kiali for observability • Verified the installation using both the OpenShift CLI and Web Console, ensuring all components are running correctly • Set up a Service Mesh Member Roll to define which namespaces participate in the service mesh • Tested the service mesh functionality by deploying a sample application with automatic sidecar injection

This installation provides the foundation for implementing advanced microservices patterns such as traffic management, security policies, and observability. The service mesh will enable you to manage complex microservices architectures with features like mutual TLS, circuit breaking, load balancing, and distributed tracing.

Understanding OpenShift Service Mesh installation is crucial for the Red Hat Certified Specialist in Building Resilient Microservices exam, as it demonstrates your ability to set up the infrastructure required for production-ready microservices deployments. The skills learned in this lab will enable you to implement robust, observable, and secure microservices architectures in enterprise environments.
